{"builder":"Make one pull request for https://github.com/o/r/issues/23 (add clamp to mathlib). It must meet every one of these acceptance lines:\n- math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\n- clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\n- README.md documents every function math.js exports\n- npm test passes\n\nCurrent state: math.js is CommonJS and exports { add, subtract, multiply } via module.exports. README.md has an \"## API\" section with a \"### name(args)\" heading, a one-line description and a short js example per function, but it documents only add and multiply. subtract is missing. Tests live in test/math.test.js and run with `npm test` (`node --test`).\n\nSteps:\n1. In math.js, add a `clamp(x, lo, hi)` function with a short doc comment like the others. If lo > hi, throw `new RangeError(...)` with a clear message. Otherwise return lo when x < lo, hi when x > hi, and x otherwise. lo === hi is a valid range. Add clamp to module.exports and keep add, subtract and multiply exported unchanged.\n2. In test/math.test.js, add tests in the existing style. Cover: a value inside the range is returned unchanged; values below and above are pinned to lo and hi (for example clamp(-5, 0, 100) === 0 and clamp(140, 0, 100) === 100); the boundaries themselves; negative ranges; lo === hi; and `assert.throws(() => clamp(5, 10, 0), RangeError)`.\n3. In README.md's \"## API\" section, add a \"### subtract(a, b)\" entry and a \"### clamp(x, lo, hi)\" entry, matching the existing heading/description/example format. The clamp entry should say that it limits x to [lo, hi] and throws a RangeError when lo > hi. After this, every function in math.js's exports must have its own \"### name(...)\" heading.\n4. Run `npm test` and make sure every test passes, including the existing ones.\n5. Commit and open the pull request. Reference issue #23 in its description.","verifier":"Check the pull request head for https://github.com/o/r/issues/23. Run every command from the repository root at the PR head. There is one check per acceptance line, in order. Report PASS or FAIL for each, with the command output as evidence.\n\nCheck 1 — expect: \"math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\"\nRun:\nnode -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); if(typeof m.clamp!=='function'){console.log('FAIL: clamp not exported');process.exit(1)} const c=[[5,0,10,5],[-5,0,100,0],[140,0,100,100],[0,0,100,0],[100,0,100,100],[3,3,3,3],[-2.5,-10,-1,-2.5],[-20,-10,-1,-10]]; let bad=0; for(const [x,lo,hi,want] of c){const got=m.clamp(x,lo,hi); if(!Object.is(got,want)){bad++;console.log('FAIL: clamp('+x+','+lo+','+hi+') = '+got+', expected '+want)}} if(bad)process.exit(1); console.log('PASS')\"\nPASS: the command prints PASS and exits 0, which means clamp is an exported function, returns x when lo <= x <= hi, lo when x < lo, and hi when x > hi. FAIL: it prints any FAIL line, exits non-zero, or throws.\n\nCheck 2 — expect: \"clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\"\nRun:\nnode -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); let bad=0; for(const [x,lo,hi] of [[5,10,0],[0,1,0.5],[50,100,0]]){try{m.clamp(x,lo,hi);bad++;console.log('FAIL: clamp('+x+','+lo+','+hi+') did not throw')}catch(e){if(!(e instanceof RangeError)){bad++;console.log('FAIL: clamp('+x+','+lo+','+hi+') threw '+(e&&e.name)+' not RangeError')}}} if(bad)process.exit(1); console.log('PASS')\"\nPASS: it prints PASS and exits 0, so every call with lo > hi threw a RangeError. FAIL: any call returned without throwing, or threw something other than a RangeError.\n\nCheck 3 — expect: \"README.md documents every function math.js exports\"\nRun:\nnode -e \"const fs=require('fs'); const m=require('./math.js'); const r=fs.readFileSync('README.md','utf8'); const names=Object.keys(m).filter(k=>typeof m[k]==='function'); const missing=names.filter(n=>!new RegExp('^#{1,6}\\\\s.*\\\\b'+n+'\\\\s*\\\\(','m').test(r)); console.log('exports: '+names.join(', ')); if(missing.length){console.log('FAIL: no README heading for: '+missing.join(', '));process.exit(1)} console.log('PASS')\"\nThe script lists the exported functions and looks for a README heading for each one. Then open README.md and confirm that each listed export (it should include add, subtract, multiply and clamp) has a description of what it does.\nPASS: every exported function is described in README.md. FAIL: any exported function, subtract included, is not documented. If the script reports a name as missing but README.md clearly documents that function in another form, judge by the README's content and say so.\n\nCheck 4 — expect: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test\nPASS: exit code 0 and the summary shows fail 0. FAIL: non-zero exit, or any failing or cancelled test.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS/FAIL):\n- Note whether test/math.test.js has new tests for clamp, both for in-range and out-of-range values and for the RangeError.\n- Note whether add, subtract and multiply are still exported and behave as before."}